Ladybird keeps each port channel in ExtensionMessageBroker, checks
every port operation against the registered context, and records every
receiver before dispatching to any. The opener’s messages wait for every
acknowledgment. A receiver that leaves, by navigating away or by calling
disconnect(), leaves the opener connected until the last receiver goes.

Chromium records the opener and receiver sides in MessageService::
OpenChannelImpl(), and ExtensionMessagePort::UnregisterFrame()
closes the port only once IsValidPort() finds no receiver left.
Ladybird applies that rule to a receiver’s explicit disconnect
as well as to its teardown.

Gecko holds port messages back with portPromises in ProxyMessenger::
recvPortConnect() until onConnect has run, as Ladybird does
with its acknowledgments. But Gecko’s recvConduitClosed() disconnects
the opposite side when any receiver closes; Ladybird keeps the opener
connected to the receivers that remain.

WebKit queues JSON messages in m_portQueuedMessages, from
WebExtensionContext::portPostMessage(), and drops a page’s ports through
disconnectPortsForPage() and m_pagePortMap. Ladybird queues opening
messages too, with a limit of 1,024, and closes both sides of every
channel when an extension is disabled, removed, or reloaded.
